<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cayenne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cayenne is the capital of French Guiana, an overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on the north-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>. French settlers established a post there in 1643, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English interludes France held it from 1676. It was the administrative centre of the colony and of its notorious penal system from 1852, and remains the territory's main town and port.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cayenne.jpg" alt="Cayenne" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cayambe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cayenne_place_des_palmistes_rue_de_Remire_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cayenne stands on a former island at the mouth of the Cayenne River on the Atlantic coast, and the city occupies part of Cayenne Island. It is 268 kilometres from Saint-Laurent-du-Maroni and 64 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kourou">Kourou</a>. Its motto is fert aurum industria, work brings wealth. The climate is tropical, with average highs of about 30 °C and lows of about 23 °C throughout the year and a very long wet season. The port of Dégrad des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> on the Mahury estuary has replaced Larivot and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iles-du-salut">Îles du Salut</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, rum and gold are exported in small quantities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Spanish explorers who first visited the area found it too hot and did not settle it. The French founded their first settlement in 1604, but the Portuguese destroyed it, determined to enforce the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a>. French colonists returned in 1643 and founded Cayenne. An Amerindian chief named Cépérou sold them the rock on which Fort Cépérou stands, and the colonists were later forced to leave by attacks from the Indigenous population. Over the following decades the colony changed hands among the French, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and English before being restored to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Cayenne became a municipality in 1790.</p><p>In 1809 an Anglo-Portuguese force captured it, and it was administered from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> until 1817, when it returned to French control. It was used as a French penal colony until 1938. The Pasteur Institute has a branch in Cayenne, which studies tropical diseases. The Botanical Garden has its origin in a garden that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> established in 1786 as a storehouse and nursery for plants, to which the director Louis Claude Richard brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nutmeg">nutmeg</a> trees, rattan, cardamom and black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> vines from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cayenne is the capital, prefecture and largest city of French Guiana, and the largest Francophone city on the South American continent. About 62,675 people live in the commune itself and about 153,884 in its metropolitan area, which has grown through immigration, chiefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, and a high birth rate. Its heritage includes Fort Cépérou, the Cathedral of Saint-Sauveur, built between 1825 and 1833 and registered as a historic monument, and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creole</a> houses. Its carnival, held between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epiphany-holiday">Epiphany</a> and Ash Wednesday, comes from Guianan Creole culture; in early colonial times enslaved people were forbidden to join the settlers' carnival and held clandestine festivals of their own.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cayenne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>French attempts to settle <b>Cayenne</b> began in the early 17th century, including one in 1643 that ended in disaster.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a></li></ul></section>
